+// zkVM guests (ZisK, SP1, OpenVM) get all their memory from one downward bump
+// pointer kept in a cell at a fixed address, shared with the guest's own C
+// allocator. Non-zero enables per-site inline allocation of fixed-size
+// reference classes against that cell instead of a call to the allocation
+// helper; the value is the LOW 32 BITS of the cell's address (every such guest
+// is mapped below 4 GiB) and must be 8-byte aligned. The cell 8 bytes BELOW it
+// must hold the lowest address the heap may occupy, published before any
+// managed code runs and never changed afterwards.
+//
+// 0 (the default) keeps the helper call. Every non-zkVM riscv64 target must
+// leave it at 0: the address is a raw absolute constant with no relocation and
+// means nothing outside the guest it was chosen for.
+RELEASE_CONFIG_INTEGER(JitZkBumpAddr,               "JitZkBumpAddr",             0)
